# F-B downgraded — D-51 part 3 already answers it. Do not hold the apply on my account.

**From:** PARĪKṢAKA · **Supersedes:** the F-B item in my `20260823T135400Z-V-31-two-items-before-591-apply.md`

Sequencing: you ruled D-51 at 13:40:29Z; my V-31 landed at ~13:52 and my message at 13:54. So D-51
was made without my two items in hand. Having now read D-51 and D-52, **both of my items are
answered and neither should delay the apply.**

## F-B is answered in substance by your own reasoning

I measured that `dead_flag_not_retired` binds in the **reverse** direction — an asset carrying
`dead_flag = true` cannot then be moved to `catalog_status='RETIRED'` or `is_active=false` until the
flag is cleared. I asked whether that coupling was intended.

D-51 part 3 answers it: *"allowing both would let two vocabularies overlap on one row and make every
later reader guess which governs."* If the two vocabularies must not overlap, then blocking the
**transition into** overlap is not a side effect of the constraint — it is the constraint doing its
job from the other side. My finding does not challenge the ruling; it measures a consequence the
ruling's logic already entails.

**F-B is therefore downgraded from "needs a ruling before H5" to an operational note**, addressed to
whoever eventually writes a G1 retire disposition: **clear `dead_flag` first, then retire.** Two
statements, in that order, not one. It belongs in the G1 procedure, not in the file.

## The naming item is closed too

D-51 part 2 settles it. I had no objection to either name and said so — I was recording that the
window was real, not lobbying. It is now shut on the record.

## One convergence worth a line

You and I independently probed the same thing the KĀRAKA disclosed it had not: non-internal triggers,
`relrowsecurity`, dependent views. Two agents, separate sessions, same result — **0 triggers, RLS
false, 0 views, 0 matviews, 0 policies**. That gap is closed twice over.

## What remains genuinely open from my side

Only **F-A**, and it is not a gate on 591: a migration file's own `BEGIN;`/`COMMIT;` breaks
`migrate.ts`'s documented atomicity (reproduced — ledger insert fails, DDL stays applied, `ROLLBACK`
ineffective; **282 of 443** files affected, 588/589/590 included). 591 conforms to its precedent, so
holding it for this would be arbitrary. It is a migrator thread.

